/**
  @page examples-density_optimization-simp_temperature_minimization Temperature minimization
  @brief Assemble a state, adjoint and regularized density update for heat conduction.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-densopt-temp-intro Domain, design and objective

  On the unit square, the material density @f$\gamma\in[0,1]@f$ controls
  the conductivity
  @f[
    a(\gamma)=a_{\min}+(a_{\max}-a_{\min})\gamma^3,
    \qquad a_{\min}=10^{-4},\quad a_{\max}=1.
  @f]
  The positive lower bound keeps the state equation elliptic at zero density.
  With unit heat source, the temperature satisfies
  @f[
    -\nabla\cdot(a(\gamma)\nabla u)=1,\qquad
    u=0\text{ on }\Gamma_D,\qquad
    a(\gamma)\partial_nu=0\text{ on the remaining boundary}.
  @f]
  The objective combines mean temperature and material usage.
  @f[
    J(\gamma)=\frac{1}{|\Omega|}\int_\Omega u_\gamma\,dx
               +\ell\int_\Omega\gamma\,dx,\qquad\ell=4.
  @f]
  Material usage is penalized with a fixed weight. The algorithm uses projected
  regularized gradient steps.

  @section examples-densopt-temp-setup Discrete setup

  @subsection examples-densopt-temp-setup-params Parameters

  The state, adjoint and density use scalar continuous `P1` spaces.
  The initial density is 0.9, the step coefficient is @f$\mu=0.01@f$ and
  the gradient regularization coefficient is @f$\alpha=0.05@f$.
  The default iteration budget is 5,000.

  @subsection examples-densopt-temp-setup-mesh Mesh and sink boundary

  A 32-by-32 vertex grid gives 1,024 vertices and 1,922 triangles. Boundary
  edges are selected as heat sinks when at least one endpoint is on the left
  boundary and within 0.1 of its midpoint. This endpoint-based rule defines
  the actual mesh-aligned sink interval.

  @snippet DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp density-mesh

  @section examples-densopt-temp-loop State, adjoint and update

  @subsection examples-densopt-temp-loop-state State equation

  At the current density, the state equation is assembled and solved.
  Factorization or solution failure terminates the program.

  @snippet DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp density-state

  @subsection examples-densopt-temp-loop-adjoint Adjoint and sensitivity

  Using the Lagrangian convention in this program, the adjoint satisfies
  @f[
    \int_\Omega a(\gamma)\nabla p\cdot\nabla q\,dx
      =-\frac{1}{|\Omega|}\int_\Omega q\,dx.
  @f]
  It has the same homogeneous essential boundary data as the state. The
  objective derivative in density direction @f$\eta@f$ is
  @f[
    J'(\gamma)[\eta]=\int_\Omega
      \left(\ell+3(a_{\max}-a_{\min})\gamma^2\nabla u\cdot\nabla p\right)\eta\,dx.
  @f]

  @snippet DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp density-adjoint

  @subsection examples-densopt-temp-loop-gradient Regularized gradient

  The derivative density is represented through
  @f[
    \alpha\int_\Omega\nabla g\cdot\nabla w\,dx
      +\int_\Omega gw\,dx=J'(\gamma)[w].
  @f]
  Zero gradient values on the sink keep the design's sink-boundary nodal
  values fixed during these updates. The coefficient convention gives a
  smoothing length @f$\sqrt{\alpha}@f$.

  @snippet DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp density-gradient

  @subsection examples-densopt-temp-loop-update Project the density

  The update is @f$\gamma^{k+1}=\Pi_{[0,1]}(\gamma^k-\mu g^k)@f$.
  The current state, adjoint, gradient and density are exported together
  before the density changes, so a snapshot represents one consistent iterate.

  @snippet DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp density-update

  The fixed step and fixed iteration budget define this demonstration.
  Assess a run through its objective, material usage and density fields.
  Objective-controlled step selection and an optimality stopping test are
  additional algorithm choices.

  @section examples-densopt-temp-output Inspect the results

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html SIMPTemperatureMinimization-density.png "Computed P1 material density at the last recorded iterate."
  @image html SIMPTemperatureMinimization-temperature.png "Computed temperature on the same design."
  @endparblock

  In the checked 5,000-state run, the objective decreased from approximately
  4.47838 to 3.16329, with no increases in the recorded history. Material usage
  decreased from 0.9 to approximately 0.53165. Each state, adjoint and gradient
  solve succeeded. These observations describe the fixed-step run.

  `TemperatureMinimization.csv` records mean temperature, material usage and
  their weighted objective. The XDMF output contains temperature **u**, adjoint
  **p**, regularized gradient **g**, density **gamma** and update **step**.
  Open `TemperatureMinimization.xdmf` in ParaView and select **Apply**.

  @section examples-densopt-temp-full Build and run

  Build `TemperatureMinimization`. The executable generates its mesh, so no
  resource file is required. Run it in a scratch directory; an optional positive
  integer argument selects the iteration budget. Omitting it runs 5,000 updates.
  The program checks each sparse solve and each recorded objective for failure.

  @ref DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp "Complete source code"
  contains the boundary selection and numerical update.

  @section examples-densopt-temp-see-also Continue

  - @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Scalar diffusion formulation"
  - @ref guides-shape-optimization "Optimization representations"
  - @ref guides-numerical-contracts "Interpolation and algebraic signs"

  @example DensityOptimization/TemperatureMinimization.cpp
  @m_footernavigation
*/
